  <text>
    <body>
      <div>
        <head>In the directories</head>
        <p>Dight's Flour Mills are in the directories from 1857 to 1880, in <ref target="civic?id=Leicester%20Street">Leicester Street</ref> just past the Queensberry Street corner, and up to 1863 also in <ref target="civic?id=Queensberry%20Street">Queensberry Street</ref>. They are listed in every year except 1864.</p>
        <p>The listings follow the mill's owners. John Dight, flour mills, in 1857 and 1858 (Dight and Co., flour merchants, in Queensberry Street in 1857). Henry Scott and Co., Dight's steam flour mills, from 1859 to 1862, at No. 37 Leicester Street to 1861 and No. 48 from 1862, and at No. 1 and then No. 11 Queensberry Street. Dight and Scott, merchants and millers, in 1863. D. and R. Gibson, Dight's Flour Mills, in 1865 and 1866. Gibson and Dight's Flour Mills in 1867 and 1868, and David Gibson, flour mills, from 1869 to 1880.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <head>Neighbours</head>
        <p>The <ref target="civic?id=Carlton%20Flour%20Mills">Carlton Flour Mills</ref> of Samuel Ramsden, and later George Ramsden, are next door from 1859 to 1880, so for twenty years two flour mills stood side by side in Leicester Street. In 1857 and 1858 the neighbours are William Davies's general store and George Edmonds's Carlton Hotel (the Carlton Inn in 1858).</p>
      </div>
